            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I don't think this was answered before, so no need to apologize :)\r\n\r\nAlthough not straightforward, there is a way to do this. The `gen_spec.treesitter()` [function returns a callable (i.e. function) specification](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/23669ab7abc2cd10f9a5166de07400bb34eaecda/doc/mini-ai.txt#L767-L770). This returned function has the same signature as [`find_textobject()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/23669ab7abc2cd10f9a5166de07400bb34eaecda/doc/mini-ai.txt#L560-L575), which in turn can be configured to use specific search method via `opts.search_method`.\r\n\r\nSo the solution here would be to wrap the output of `gen_spec.treesitter()` in own function which forces specific search method. Here is an example for \"function definition\" textobject:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nlocal ai = require('mini.ai')\r\nlocal spec_ts = ai.gen_spec.treesitter({ a = '@function.outer', i = '@function.inner' })\r\nlocal spec_ts_cover = function(ai_type, id, opts)\r\n  opts.search_method = 'cover'\r\n  return spec_ts(ai_type, id, opts)\r\nend\r\nai.setup({\r\n  custom_textobjects = {\r\n    F = spec_ts_cover,\r\n  },\r\n})\r\n```\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nThat said, my recommendation would be to use `'cover'` method as the main one and explicitly use `an` / `al` / `in` / `il` variants if you want next/previous textobject.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I don't think this was answered before, so no need to apologize :)</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Although not straightforward, there is a way to do this. The <code class=\"notranslate\">gen_spec.treesitter()</code> <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/23669ab7abc2cd10f9a5166de07400bb34eaecda/doc/mini-ai.txt#L767-L770\">function returns a callable (i.e. function) specification</a>. This returned function has the same signature as <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/23669ab7abc2cd10f9a5166de07400bb34eaecda/doc/mini-ai.txt#L560-L575\"><code class=\"notranslate\">find_textobject()</code></a>, which in turn can be configured to use specific search method via <code class=\"notranslate\">opts.search_method</code>.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">So the solution here would be to wrap the output of <code class=\"notranslate\">gen_spec.treesitter()</code> in own function which forces specific search method. Here is an example for \"function definition\" textobject:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"local ai = require('mini.ai')\nlocal spec_ts = ai.gen_spec.treesitter({ a = '@function.outer', i = '@function.inner' })\nlocal spec_ts_cover = function(ai_type, id, opts)\n  opts.search_method = 'cover'\n  return spec_ts(ai_type, id, opts)\nend\nai.setup({\n  custom_textobjects = {\n    F = spec_ts_cover,\n  },\n})\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">ai</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">require</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>mini.ai<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>)\n<span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">spec_ts</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">ai</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">gen_spec</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">treesitter</span>({ <span class=\"pl-smi\">a</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>@function.outer<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">i</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>@function.inner<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span>})\n<span class=\"pl-k\">local</span> <span class=\"pl-en\">spec_ts_cover</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">ai_type</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">id</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">opts</span>)\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">opts</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">search_method</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>cover<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>\n  <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">spec_ts</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">ai_type</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">id</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">opts</span>)\n<span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n<span class=\"pl-smi\">ai</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">setup</span>({\n  <span class=\"pl-smi\">custom_textobjects</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> {\n    <span class=\"pl-smi\">F</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">spec_ts_cover</span>,\n  },\n})</pre></div>\n<hr>\n<p dir=\"auto\">That said, my recommendation would be to use <code class=\"notranslate\">'cover'</code> method as the main one and explicitly use <code class=\"notranslate\">an</code> / <code class=\"notranslate\">al</code> / <code class=\"notranslate\">in</code> / <code class=\"notranslate\">il</code> variants if you want next/previous textobject.</p>",
            "bodyText": "I don't think this was answered before, so no need to apologize :)\nAlthough not straightforward, there is a way to do this. The gen_spec.treesitter() function returns a callable (i.e. function) specification. This returned function has the same signature as find_textobject(), which in turn can be configured to use specific search method via opts.search_method.\nSo the solution here would be to wrap the output of gen_spec.treesitter() in own function which forces specific search method. Here is an example for \"function definition\" textobject:\nlocal ai = require('mini.ai')\nlocal spec_ts = ai.gen_spec.treesitter({ a = '@function.outer', i = '@function.inner' })\nlocal spec_ts_cover = function(ai_type, id, opts)\n  opts.search_method = 'cover'\n  return spec_ts(ai_type, id, opts)\nend\nai.setup({\n  custom_textobjects = {\n    F = spec_ts_cover,\n  },\n})\n\nThat said, my recommendation would be to use 'cover' method as the main one and explicitly use an / al / in / il variants if you want next/previous textobject.",
