            "authorAssociation": "NONE",
            "body": "Sorry for resurrect this thread. I want to achieve the same thing but for brackets ((, [, {). I want the bracket to always use the `cover` strategy. I tried to mimic your solution but it seems like the gen_spec.pair doesn't support that\r\n\r\n```\r\n      local spec_pair = require(\"mini.ai\").gen_spec.pair\r\n      local opts = {\r\n        custom_textobjects = {\r\n          [\")\"] = spec_pair(\"(\", \")\", { type = \"balanced\", search_method = \"cover\" }),\r\n          [\"(\"] = spec_pair(\"(\", \")\", { type = \"balanced\", search_method = \"cover\" })\r\n        },\r\n      }\r\n\r\n      require(\"mini.ai\").setup(opts)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOne solution I can think of is to increase the size of `n_lines` to a sufficiently large number, like 100. I just want to know if there's any official solution before using it as the last resort.\r\n\r\nThanks for this module. I think the API is well thought-out and the documentation is superb.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Sorry for resurrect this thread. I want to achieve the same thing but for brackets ((, [, {). I want the bracket to always use the <code class=\"notranslate\">cover</code> strategy. I tried to mimic your solution but it seems like the gen_spec.pair doesn't support that</p>\n<div class=\"snippet-clipboard-content notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"      local spec_pair = require(&quot;mini.ai&quot;).gen_spec.pair\n      local opts = {\n        custom_textobjects = {\n          [&quot;)&quot;] = spec_pair(&quot;(&quot;, &quot;)&quot;, { type = &quot;balanced&quot;, search_method = &quot;cover&quot; }),\n          [&quot;(&quot;] = spec_pair(&quot;(&quot;, &quot;)&quot;, { type = &quot;balanced&quot;, search_method = &quot;cover&quot; })\n        },\n      }\n\n      require(&quot;mini.ai&quot;).setup(opts)\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><code class=\"notranslate\">      local spec_pair = require(\"mini.ai\").gen_spec.pair\n      local opts = {\n        custom_textobjects = {\n          [\")\"] = spec_pair(\"(\", \")\", { type = \"balanced\", search_method = \"cover\" }),\n          [\"(\"] = spec_pair(\"(\", \")\", { type = \"balanced\", search_method = \"cover\" })\n        },\n      }\n\n      require(\"mini.ai\").setup(opts)\n</code></pre></div>\n<p dir=\"auto\">One solution I can think of is to increase the size of <code class=\"notranslate\">n_lines</code> to a sufficiently large number, like 100. I just want to know if there's any official solution before using it as the last resort.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Thanks for this module. I think the API is well thought-out and the documentation is superb.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Sorry for resurrect this thread. I want to achieve the same thing but for brackets ((, [, {). I want the bracket to always use the cover strategy. I tried to mimic your solution but it seems like the gen_spec.pair doesn't support that\n      local spec_pair = require(\"mini.ai\").gen_spec.pair\n      local opts = {\n        custom_textobjects = {\n          [\")\"] = spec_pair(\"(\", \")\", { type = \"balanced\", search_method = \"cover\" }),\n          [\"(\"] = spec_pair(\"(\", \")\", { type = \"balanced\", search_method = \"cover\" })\n        },\n      }\n\n      require(\"mini.ai\").setup(opts)\n\nOne solution I can think of is to increase the size of n_lines to a sufficiently large number, like 100. I just want to know if there's any official solution before using it as the last resort.\nThanks for this module. I think the API is well thought-out and the documentation is superb.",
